.TH UNIXD 4
.SH NAME
unixd \- pipes and unix socket pairs for libposix (port "unix")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.BR unix .
.B /bin/unixd
is started on demand by
.BR init (8),
and stopped after 10 minutes when no end is held.
.SH DESCRIPTION
What Linux's kernel does for programs built on libposix (the POSIX layer
of the web browser), a server does here:
.TP
.B ends
each pipe or socket pair is two ends; a process holds an end (the creator,
one it was passed to, a child it was handed to); an end closes when nobody
holds it any more.
.TP
.B channels
bytes (or messages: UNIX_SEQPACKET) flowing one way: a pipe has one, a
socket pair two. A reader waits while there is nothing and a writer remains;
a writer while 256 KiB wait unread.
.TP
.B passing
a message may carry descriptors: other ends, or shared memory objects.
unixd holds them on the way and gives them to the reader.
.TP
.B stashes
what a program hands to one it starts (posix_spawn): ends, shared memory
and a block of bytes, claimed by the child with a random cookie.
.PP
One thread, one message at a time; calls that must wait are answered later.
Every 2 s it looks for holders that ended without saying so.
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B UNIX_PAIR
w[1] = UNIX_STREAM, UNIX_SEQPACKET or UNIX_PIPE \-> 0; reply w[1], w[2] = the
two ends (a pipe: w[1] reads, w[2] writes).
.TP
.B UNIX_SEND
w[1] = end, w[2] = n descriptors, w[3] = flags (UNIX_NONBLOCK); sbuf = n
unix_desc_t, then the data \-> bytes taken.
.TP
.B UNIX_RECV
w[1] = end, w[2] = most bytes, w[3] = flags (UNIX_NONBLOCK, UNIX_PEEK); rbuf
<\- descriptors, then data \-> bytes; reply w[1] = n descriptors (0 and 0:
the end).
.TP
.BR UNIX_CLOSE ", " UNIX_SHUTDOWN
hold an end once less; stop reading (0), writing (1) or both (2).
.TP
.B UNIX_POLL
sbuf = end numbers, each with what is waited for in its top byte; rbuf <\- a
byte each (NET_IN, NET_OUT, NET_ERR) \-> how many are ready; none: the
caller is noted
.RB ( sys_note (2))
later.
.TP
.BR UNIX_STASH ", " UNIX_CLAIM ", " UNIX_UNSTASH
hand descriptors and bytes to a program being started (reply w[1]: a
cookie); claim them with the cookie; give them back.
.TP
.B UNIX_BYE
the caller ends: every end it holds is closed.
.SH SEE ALSO
